**Dependencies (Spring Boot 3.x)**
- `spring-boot-starter-data-r2dbc` — Spring Data R2DBC
- `r2dbc-postgresql` — driver
- `flyway-core` — migrations still run on JDBC

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-r2dbc</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
    <artifactId>r2dbc-postgresql</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
    <artifactId>flyway-core</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

**`application.yml`**
- R2DBC for runtime queries
- JDBC URL kept for Flyway migrations

```yaml
spring:
  r2dbc:
    url: r2dbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/mydb
    username: app
    password: ${DB_PASSWORD}
    pool:
      initial-size: 5
      max-size: 20
      max-idle-time: 30m
  flyway:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/mydb
    user: app
    password: ${DB_PASSWORD}
```

**Entity**
- Use Spring Data Relational annotations — `@Table`, `@Column`, `@Id`
- No JPA annotations
- Records work cleanly

```java
@Table("orders")
public record Order(
    @Id Long id,
    @Column("customer_id") Long customerId,
    String status,
    BigDecimal amount,
    @Column("created_at") Instant createdAt) {}
```

**Repository**
- Extends `ReactiveCrudRepository`
- Method-name queries; `@Query` for explicit SQL; `@Modifying` for updates

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends ReactiveCrudRepository<Order, Long> {

    Flux<Order> findByCustomerId(Long customerId);

    @Query("SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = :status AND created_at > :since")
    Flux<Order> findRecentByStatus(String status, Instant since);

    @Modifying
    @Query("UPDATE orders SET status = :status WHERE id = :id")
    Mono<Integer> updateStatus(Long id, String status);
}
```

**Service composition**
- `flatMap` to chain async lookups
- Beware N+1 — prefer SQL `JOIN` for many-row composition

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository repo;
    private final CustomerRepository customerRepo;

    public Flux<OrderWithCustomer> findRecentWithCustomer(Instant since) {
        return repo.findRecentByStatus("PAID", since)
            .flatMap(order -> customerRepo.findById(order.customerId())
                .map(c -> new OrderWithCustomer(order, c)));
    }
}
```

**Streaming large result sets**
- `repo.findAll()` streams rows; the entire result is never loaded into memory
- Backpressure flows from the consumer to the driver to the database

```java
public Flux<Order> streamAll() {
    return repo.findAll();
}
```

**Declarative `@Transactional`**
- Auto-configured `R2dbcTransactionManager` when `spring-boot-starter-data-r2dbc` is on the classpath
- All operations in the chain run in one reactive transaction
- Commit on success; rollback on `onError`

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {

    @Transactional
    public Mono<Payment> charge(String orderId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return orderRepo.findById(orderId)
            .flatMap(order -> paymentRepo.save(new Payment(order.id(), amount)))
            .flatMap(p -> orderRepo.updateStatus(p.orderId(), "PAID").thenReturn(p));
    }
}
```

**Programmatic with `TransactionalOperator`**
- Apply at the end of the chain via `.as(tx::transactional)`
- Useful when you can't use the proxy-based `@Transactional`

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final TransactionalOperator tx;

    public Mono<Payment> charge(String orderId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return orderRepo.findById(orderId)
            .flatMap(order -> paymentRepo.save(new Payment(order.id(), amount)))
            .flatMap(p -> orderRepo.updateStatus(p.orderId(), "PAID").thenReturn(p))
            .as(tx::transactional);
    }
}
```

**`DatabaseClient` for fluent SQL**
- Best for analytical queries and joins where you want full control
- Bind parameters by name; map rows manually

```java
Flux<OrderSummary> byDay = databaseClient.sql("""
        SELECT DATE(created_at) AS day, COUNT(*) AS cnt, SUM(amount) AS total
        FROM orders
        WHERE created_at >= :from
        GROUP BY day
        """)
    .bind("from", startDate)
    .map((row, meta) -> new OrderSummary(
        row.get("day", LocalDate.class),
        row.get("cnt", Long.class),
        row.get("total", BigDecimal.class)))
    .all();
```

**`R2dbcEntityTemplate` for programmatic CRUD**
- Type-safe builder; good middle ground

```java
Mono<Order> saved = template.insert(order);
Flux<Order> recent = template.select(Order.class)
    .matching(Query.query(where("status").is("PAID"))
        .sort(Sort.by("created_at").descending())
        .limit(100))
    .all();
```

**Manual relationships**
- No automatic `@OneToMany` loading; combine with `Mono.zip`
- For large parent-child loads, use a SQL `JOIN` via `DatabaseClient` and map manually to avoid N+1

```java
public Mono<OrderWithLines> findWithLines(Long id) {
    Mono<Order> orderMono = repo.findById(id);
    Mono<List<OrderLine>> linesMono = lineRepo.findByOrderId(id).collectList();
    return Mono.zip(orderMono, linesMono)
        .map(t -> new OrderWithLines(t.getT1(), t.getT2()));
}
```

**PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY — a use case only R2DBC handles cleanly**

```java
@Autowired ConnectionFactory factory;

public Flux<String> orderEvents() {
    return Mono.from(factory.create())
        .cast(PostgresqlConnection.class)
        .flatMapMany(conn ->
            conn.createStatement("LISTEN order_events").execute()
                .thenMany(conn.getNotifications())
                .map(n -> n.getParameter())
                .doFinally(s -> conn.close().subscribe()));
}
```

The same simple query through each API.

**JDBC + Spring `JdbcTemplate`**

```java
public List<Order> findByStatus(String status) {
    return jdbc.query(
        "SELECT id, customer_id, status, amount FROM orders WHERE status = ?",
        new Object[]{status},
        (rs, i) -> new Order(
            rs.getLong("id"),
            rs.getLong("customer_id"),
            rs.getString("status"),
            rs.getBigDecimal("amount")));
}
```

**JDBC + JPA**

```java
public List<Order> findByStatus(String status) {
    return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT o FROM Order o WHERE o.status = :status", Order.class)
        .setParameter("status", status)
        .getResultList();
}
```

**R2DBC + Spring Data `ReactiveCrudRepository`**

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends ReactiveCrudRepository<Order, Long> {
    Flux<Order> findByStatus(String status);
}
```

**R2DBC + `DatabaseClient`**

```java
public Flux<Order> findByStatus(String status) {
    return databaseClient.sql(
            "SELECT id, customer_id, status, amount FROM orders WHERE status = :s")
        .bind("s", status)
        .map((row, meta) -> new Order(
            row.get("id", Long.class),
            row.get("customer_id", Long.class),
            row.get("status", String.class),
            row.get("amount", BigDecimal.class)))
        .all();
}
```

**Observations**
- JPA gives the cleanest API at the cost of cache and lazy-loading complexity
- `JdbcTemplate` is similarly clean and very explicit
- R2DBC's repository is just as clean for simple queries
- `DatabaseClient` sits at the same level as `JdbcTemplate` — explicit SQL, type-mapped rows
